2202		This is a formalization-only estimate for the last paragraph of the proof
2203		of Lemma~\ref{lem:qld-4-7}. Let $T$ be any complete measurement on extended
2204		polynomials, $R_p=T_{\mathrm{combine}_p}$ its restriction to polynomial
2205		pairs, and $C$ its completion at a fixed pair $p_0$. Projectivity is not
2206		required for this estimate. Let $U$ be a nonempty finite question set,
2207		$e_u$ a map from polynomial pairs to a finite answer alphabet, and
2208		$\{B^u_a\}_a$ a complete measurement on the opposite tensor factor.
2209		For every vector $\psi$, the consistency defect satisfies
2210		\[
2211			\E_{u\in U}\sum_{a\ne b}
2212			\langle\psi|C_{[e_u=a]}\otimes B^u_b|\psi\rangle
2213			\le \|\psi\|^2-
2214			\E_{u\in U}\sum_p
2215			\langle\psi|R_p\otimes B^u_{e_u(p)}|\psi\rangle.
2216		\]
2217		The same inequality holds pointwise for each $u$. It does not assert a
2218		lower bound for the retained overlap on the right-hand side.
2219	\end{lemma}
2220	\begin{proof}
2221		\leanok
2222		\uses{lem:qld-combined-pair-completion}
2223		Each difference $C_p-R_p$ is positive semidefinite. Tensoring it with
2224		$B^u_{e_u(p)}$ therefore gives a nonnegative quadratic form, so
2225		completion increases the diagonal overlap. Since $C$ and $B^u$ are
2226		complete, their off-diagonal overlap equals $\|\psi\|^2$ minus their
2227		diagonal overlap. Postprocessing groups the latter by the fibers of $e_u$.
2228		Apply the resulting inequality and average over $u$.
2229	\end{proof}
